From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 02:08:46 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] um: keep asynchronous host signals off Darwin SME carriers

XNU delivers a signal to a thread in streaming mode without leaving
it, and the return from the handler loses the upper bits of the Z
registers. When the Darwin host implements SME for the run, a CPU
carrier may be running translated streaming code at any moment, so no
asynchronous signal may reach it.

Carriers therefore block SIGUSR1, the IPI wake signal, except inside
the idle pselect(), whose mask already unblocks it, and block the
process-directed SIGINT, SIGTERM, SIGHUP, SIGQUIT and SIGCHLD from the
moment they start, so the process takes those on a thread that runs no
guest: the launcher, or a thread of the embedding host.

The wake signal is only an edge. Every other carrier wait polls the
pending IPIs or does not rely on its EINTR: vhost-user polling retries
EINTR, futex waits use condition variables and the pre-cooperative
pause() runs before the carriers exist.

Without SME the masks are unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 arch/um/os-Darwin/smp.c | 42 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 40 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/um/os-Darwin/smp.c b/arch/um/os-Darwin/smp.c
index ccb63c8552d74dd9111197b20604b8035c5181d7..eeac8ec76eea0b72406a3a85cf4b08bf8c38ce9d 100644
--- a/arch/um/os-Darwin/smp.c
+++ b/arch/um/os-Darwin/smp.c
@@ -14,6 +14,7 @@
 #include <os.h>
 #include <skas.h>
 #include <smp.h>
+#include "bridge.h"
 #include "internal.h"
 
 struct cpu_thread_data {
@@ -490,6 +491,40 @@ int os_send_ipi(int cpu, int vector)
 	return pipe_error != 0 ? pipe_error : -error;
 }
 
+/*
+ * XNU delivers a signal to a thread in streaming mode without leaving it, and
+ * the return loses the upper bits of the Z registers, so when the run
+ * implements SME no asynchronous signal may reach a carrier while it can run
+ * translated streaming code.  The IPI wake signal stays blocked on carriers
+ * except inside the idle pselect(), whose mask unblocks it; it is only a wake
+ * edge, and every other carrier wait polls the pending IPIs or does not rely
+ * on its EINTR.  The process-directed signals stay blocked on carriers from
+ * their creation, so the process takes them on a thread that runs no guest:
+ * the launcher, or a thread of the embedding host.
+ */
+static int carriers_block_async_signals(void)
+{
+#ifdef CONFIG_UML_ARM64_SME
+	return darwin_uml_guest_sme_vector_length() != 0;
+#else
+	return 0;
+#endif
+}
+
+static void add_carrier_blocked_signals(sigset_t *set)
+{
+	static const int process_signals[] = {
+		DARWIN_IPI_SIGNAL, SIGINT, SIGTERM, SIGHUP, SIGQUIT, SIGCHLD,
+	};
+	unsigned int index;
+
+	if (!carriers_block_async_signals())
+		return;
+	for (index = 0; index < sizeof(process_signals) /
+		     sizeof(process_signals[0]); index++)
+		sigaddset(set, process_signals[index]);
+}
+
 static void *cpu_carrier(void *argument)
 {
 	struct cpu_thread_data *data = argument;
@@ -554,6 +589,7 @@ static int start_cpu_carrier(int cpu, int primary, void *stack,
 	}
 	sigemptyset(&block);
 	sigaddset(&block, SIGALRM);
+	add_carrier_blocked_signals(&block);
 	error = pthread_sigmask(SIG_BLOCK, &block, &previous);
 	if (error == 0) {
 		mask_set = 1;
@@ -662,8 +698,10 @@ void os_init_smp(void)
 		panic("Darwin: unable to install IPI handler: %d", errno);
 	sigemptyset(&unblock);
 	sigaddset(&unblock, DARWIN_IPI_SIGNAL);
-	if (pthread_sigmask(SIG_UNBLOCK, &unblock, NULL) != 0)
-		panic("Darwin: unable to unblock IPI wake signal");
+	/* CPU0's carrier runs this; later carriers inherit its mask. */
+	if (pthread_sigmask(carriers_block_async_signals() ? SIG_BLOCK :
+			    SIG_UNBLOCK, &unblock, NULL) != 0)
+		panic("Darwin: unable to set the IPI wake signal mask");
 	darwin_set_current_cpu(0);
 }
 
